In article <JRM.91Jul12085641@gamma.ma.adfa.oz.au> jrm@gamma.ma.adfa.oz.au (John R Marley) writes:

>   > I was trying to convert my neutral Wizard to lawful the other day.  So I
>   > pray once - my God is unhappy, I pray twice - my God takes me down a level,
>   > I pray three times - my God surrounds me with a malignant aura.  I figure
>   > by this time he probably plenty mad at me, so I make a sacrifice at the
>   > lawful altar, but instead of converting me to lawful there is a conflict
>   > between my God and the lawful God and the lawful God wins.  So I sacrifice 
>   > again and the altar get converted to neutral.  
>   > What's the deal?  How angry does my God need to be for me to get converted
>   > to another alignment? 
>
>   Ah!  It's not only making your god angry that does the trick it's also what you
>   sacrifice!

>   To change your alignment rather than just the altar, sacrifice a unicorn
>   corpse of your old alignment.  So, in your case, you get your Neutral god
>   angry, and then sacrifice a grey (gray?) unicorn corpse on the lawful altar.
>   That ought to do it.

Well, it doesn't *have* to be a unicorn. My preferred method is to
kick my dog to death and then offer the corpse at the altar I wish to
convert to. And even if my pet doesn't leave a corpse, any old (well,
fresh actually) corpse will do, such as a newt. It seems that killing
your pet makes your god sufficiently angry, without losing levels or
more then 1 point of luck.

